221: not competing final ledgers: $P^{+}_{6}$ is the exact six-row current-package
222: certificate state used by Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness},
223: while $P^{+}_{7}$ is the submitted theorem-ledger extension used by
224: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.
225: \end{theorem}
226: 
227: \begin{proof}
228: The tables and support records are finite, so the assertion is checked by
229: coordinates.  By definition, $P^{+}_{6}$ contains the primary article, this
230: supplement, the six accepted rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, their certificate fields, and
231: the conditional review-bundle support surface named in
232: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}.  The state
233: $P^{+}_{7}$ is formed by adding one additional row, $a_7$, together with the
234: manifest exposure of \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  No row $a_i$ with
235: $1\leq i\leq 6$ is edited in this construction, and no field of its certificate
236: is replaced: the same finite tuple $(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)$ is read by
237: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} in both states.  Hence
238: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} gives the same acceptance decision
239: for each of the six rows in both states.
240: 
241: The added coordinate has a different declared role.  Its accepted claim is not a
242: new mathematical proof, a proof-assistant rebuild, or an extractor-completeness
243: claim; it is the obligation-ledger traceability claim with syntactic label coverage
244: later classified in Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}.  Since the finite
245: record added to pass from $P^{+}_{6}$ to $P^{+}_{7}$ is exactly this new row and
246: its manifest exposure, every accepted claim outside $a_7$ is inherited unchanged
247: from $P^{+}_{6}$, while $a_7$ supplies only the bounded theorem-ledger coordinate.
248: This proves both the nesting and the non-competition statement.
249: \end{proof}
250: 
251: The setting is adjacent to proof-assistant systems such as Lean
252: \cite{deMouraKADR2015Lean}, machine-learning interfaces to formal proving such
253: as LeanDojo \cite{YangEtAl2023LeanDojo}, informal-to-formal guidance methods
254: such as Draft--Sketch--Prove \cite{JiangEtAl2023DraftSketchProve}, and
255: large-scale formal-proof archives such as the Archive of Formal Proofs
256: \cite{BlanchetteHMN2015AFP}.  Our focus is different: we study the
257: publication-facing evidence discipline around such sources.
258: 
259: \section{Architecture}
260: 
261: The workflow separates source work from publication work, and the separation is
262: the organizing contract for the rest of the supplement.  BEDC source files are
263: treated as finite-kernel theory together with proof obligations and separate
264: implementation contracts.  Lean and related markers are formal-verification
265: records for named obligations, not object-language closure by themselves.
266: Finite manifests, evaluator rows, bundled snapshots, and command logs are the
267: substrate witnesses from which publication certificates are built.  AI agents
268: remain advisory: they may propose, review, and triage, but they do not promote
269: claims into accepted theory.  The automath layer is therefore a publication
270: compiler: it packages the source-theory interface, finite witnesses, route state,
271: and submission boundary without merging those evidence axes.
272: 
273: \begin{definition}[Architecture-axis map]
274: \label{def:architecture-axis-map}
275: The compiler architecture uses five disjoint evidence axes.  An accepted claim may
276: read evidence from an axis only at the admissible evidence level shown in
277: the displayed axis map; the negative boundary in the last column
278: is part of the claim certificate and cannot be discharged by prose from another
279: axis.
280: \begin{center}
281: \small
282: \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{p{.16\textwidth}p{.27\textwidth}p{.27\textwidth}X}
283: \toprule
284: Axis & Role in this paper & Admissible evidence & Negative boundary \\
285: \midrule
286: BEDC source theory & finite-kernel theory, proof obligations, and implementation
287: contracts & pinned source paths, commits, imported labels, bounded summaries, and
288: source-interface records & not a publication acceptance record, not a fresh rebuild,
289: and not a complete BEDC exposition \\
290: Lean markers & formal-verification records for named obligations & recorded build,
291: axiom-purity, marker-existence, or source-interface rows when named by the package &
292: not object-language closure, not discovery by itself, and not proof of unrelated
293: claims \\
294: Finite witnesses & manifest/evaluator substrate evidence for artifacts and package
295: certificates & manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, bundled snapshots, verifier logs,
296: file digests, and limitation ledgers & not semantic universality, not whole-tree
297: dynamic validation, and not an unbounded artifact rerun \\
298: Agents & proposal, search, review, ranking, and repair assistance & advisory drafts,
299: review reports, scheduler scores, and gate-input suggestions after being recorded
300: as such & not proof evidence, not human promotion, not novelty certification, and
301: not gate passage \\
302: Automath publication compiler & intake, route, overlap, submission-pack, and support
303: surface governance & promotion records, active-paper markers, overlap ledgers,
304: submission-interface maps, digest manifests, command logs, and upload-or-archive
305: records when present & not the whole mathematical contribution, not venue
306: acceptance, and not authority to merge evidence axes \\
307: \bottomrule
308: \end{tabularx}
309: \end{center}
310: \end{definition}
311: 
312: \begin{proposition}[Axis non-escalation]
313: \label{prop:architecture-axis-non-escalation}
314: In any record-gate-sound package using Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map},
315: passage or evidence on one architecture axis cannot certify a load-bearing claim
316: whose claim kind requires another axis.  More precisely, if a claim certificate
317: uses an axis $A$ but its stronger reading needs an evidence item or negative-boundary
318: discharge listed only on a different axis $A'$, then the stronger reading is not
319: accepted unless the package contains a separate submitted coordinate for $A'$ and
320: the gates assigned to that coordinate pass.  Consequently
321: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs only finite publication
322: certificates whose axis coordinates are explicitly present in the submitted support
323: surface; it does not infer BEDC closure from Lean markers, artifact semantics from
324: manifest rows, proof evidence from agents, venue upload from automath route state,
325: or support-surface availability from local bundle paths.
326: \end{proposition}
327: 
328: \begin{proof}
329: The operational interface records a claim by finite coordinates: claim kind,
330: evidence surface, evidence level, pass records assigned by $\Gamma$, and the
331: boundary field.  Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map} makes the axis of each
332: such coordinate explicit and declares the corresponding negative boundary.  If a
333: stronger reading needs an item on a different axis $A'$, then either the claim-kind
334: coordinate does not name the kind requiring $A'$, the evidence surface does not
335: contain an admissible $A'$ item, a required $A'$ pass record is missing, or the
336: boundary field still excludes the stronger reading.  In all four cases the finite
337: record verifier of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
338: rejects the stronger reading: the dispatch table cannot assign an unmentioned gate,
339: the surface check cannot read an unsubmitted path or log, the pass-record check
340: cannot be satisfied without the row, and the boundary check preserves the declared
341: negative boundary.
342: 
343: Conversely, when a separate coordinate for $A'$ is submitted, it is just another
344: finite component of the package.  It can be accepted only by the same schema:
345: its claim kind must dispatch to the appropriate gates, its evidence surface must
346: list the admissible $A'$ evidence, and its boundary must state exactly what is and
347: is not certified.  Therefore evidence never moves between axes by paraphrase or by
348: agent-generated text; it moves only by adding an explicit finite coordinate and
349: passing the assigned gates.  Applying this to the five rows of
350: Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map} gives the listed consequences.  They are
351: also the axis-level instance of the coordinate-determination result later isolated
352: in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}: facts outside the
353: finite coordinates of the accepted package are not conclusions of the certificate.
354: \end{proof}
355: The reader path is therefore architectural before it is logistical.  The rest of
356: the supplement first fixes the source-interface spine, then defines the finite
357: record verifier, then proves the certificate construction and non-escalation
358: boundary, then instantiates that boundary on the current six-row package, and
359: only then lists the local case snapshots and command-run inventory.  File tables
360: are included as finite witnesses for this interface, not as a substitute for the
361: compiler architecture.
362: 
363: \begin{center}
364: \small
365: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.22\linewidth}p{0.08\linewidth}p{0.22\linewidth}p{0.08\linewidth}X}
366: \toprule
367: Input layer & & Compiler boundary & & Output layer \\
368: \midrule
369: Pinned source paths, bounded source-interface rows, finite manifests, case
370: snapshots, command logs when present, gate-pass rows, and human-promotion records
371: & $\longrightarrow$ & finite certificate constructor plus schema verifier
372: $(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B;S,L,\Gamma)$ & $\longrightarrow$ & accepted finite
373: publication certificates and finite bad subrecords, each with an explicit
374: negative boundary \\
375: \bottomrule
376: \end{tabularx}
377: \end{center}
378: 
379: Concretely, the newmath source workspace contains Lean files, scripts, and
380: artifact sources.  The automath publication workspace contains intake packets,
381: active paper tracks, review state, and submission metadata.  A candidate first
382: enters an intake seed.  It becomes an active paper only after a human promotion
383: decision creates a \texttt{2026\_*} directory with \texttt{main.tex} and
384: \texttt{PIPELINE.md}.  This prevents source organization from being mistaken for
385: a submission route.
386: 
387: The source side supplies a discovery-gate calculus.  In this calculus,
388: statement-only and code-only candidates are insufficient; mechanical expansion
389: and compression are not discovery by themselves; discovery claims require a
390: semantic certificate and a ledger witness; demotion preserves source-interface
391: audit soundness;
392: accepted claims must form an auditable lineage DAG; and a pipeline is safe only
393: when discovery claims pass the gate before being recorded as accepted work.
